A NOTE FOR THE NEXT RE-RUN. SFlowVizWorkspace.cpp has changed since d1010c7:
#48 rewrote PushToVolume to push the transfer function alongside the clip. All
six anchors here were re-checked against that HEAD -- parsed through
parse_mutants.py and counted against the file, rather than eyeballing their
first lines -- and each still matches exactly once, so every arm still applies.

apply_mutant already refuses both failure modes (exit 3 absent, exit 4
ambiguous), so a drifted anchor SKIPs rather than mutating the wrong site. The
hazard is not a bad mutation; it is that SKIP is neither SURVIVED nor killed.
mutate.sh prints the skip per-arm and carries `skipped N` in its summary, but
its EXIT CODE reads only SURVIVED and UNSCORED -- so a campaign whose every arm
drifted would print `killed 0 ... skipped 6` and exit 0, the same code as a
clean sweep. An arm that stops applying stops testing anything, and the
killed-count does not fall when it happens: it just has fewer terms.

So read the skip count, not the exit status. That is why the verdict tables
above carry their skipped column explicitly (0 in both runs, all six arms
applied).

Arm 1 is the identity control and must SURVIVE. If it ever does not, the harness
is scoring noise and every line above is void.

The rationale lives here rather than at the top of the .txt because
parse_mutants.py splits on `%%` and raises ValueError on any record that is not
exactly three `--`-separated fields. A prose header would abort the campaign.
